# geometry/index.html
seg-cd545b3588cf = <b>Dilution of precision (DOP)</b> is how much the satellites' geometry multiplies the error of each range measurement. Satellites spread across the sky give low DOP; satellites bunched together, or all high overhead, give high DOP. GDOP is the geometric DOP (position and time), PDOP the three-dimensional position DOP, HDOP horizontal, VDOP vertical and TDOP the receiver clock (time) DOP.

# lunar/index.html
# c1: lunar-station-coverage
seg-ce9444c95be8 = A differential reference station at a surveyed site computes corrections for the lunar navigation satellites it sees; a user nearby applies them, and the errors the two share cancel. This view maps the error that is left over a south-polar grid and a day of epochs: the corrected percentile error per point, how far from the station it stays within a threshold, availability and protection levels. Every figure comes from the Kshana engine's <c1/> report. <a1>What this shows</a1>.

# geometry/index.html
seg-cf1312ed607a = <b>Position error ≈ σ<s1>UERE</s1> × DOP.</b> The user-equivalent range error (σ<s2>UERE</s2>) is the one-sigma error of each range: satellite clock and orbit, the atmosphere, multipath and receiver noise together. Multiplying it by HDOP gives the one-sigma horizontal error, and by VDOP the vertical.

